of f.\nProof. That the real roots of such polynomials are in the interval [−d, d −1] was\nshown in [2]. We prove that the theorem holds for complex values in C1\nd with positive\n35--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTitle Author(s) | Enumerative and algebraic invariants of lattice polytopes Koelbl, Max --- | --- Title Citation | Enumerative and algebraic invariants of lattice polytopes 大阪大学, 2025, 博士論文 Title Version Type | Enumerative and algebraic invariants of lattice polytopes VoR Title URL | Enumerative and algebraic invariants of lattice polytopes https://doi.org/10.18910/101775 Title rights | Enumerative and algebraic invariants of lattice polytopes Title Note | Enumerative and algebraic invariants of lattice polytopesThe University of Osaka Institutional Knowledge Archive : OUKA (https://ir.library.osaka-u.ac.jp/repo/ouka/all/101775/34955_Abstract.pdf)\nciteturn12search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; The main results characterises the space of roots attainable by SNN-polynomials under the condition that all roots have real part -1/2. This confirms a special case of a conjecture of Braun and Develin (2006).\nThe second chapter serves as an introduction to equivariant Ehrhart theory by supplying some background in representation theory and detailing the equivariant versions of Ehrhart theoretic notions. The second part revolves around the question “What can we know about lattice polytopes whose Ehrhart polynomial roots have real part -1/2?”. This part contains two chapters. The first chapter is a general investigation of a class of Polynomials – Stanley non-negative (SNN) polynomials -- which includes all Ehrhart polynomials. The main results characterises the space of roots attainable by SNN-polynomials under the condition that all roots have real part -1/2. This confirms a special case of a conjecture of Braun and Develin (2006). The second chapter revolves around symmetric edge polytopes (SEPs) – a type of polytopes that are defined by graphs. Higashitani, Kummer, and Michałek conjectured in (2017) that SEPs defined by complete mulitpartite graphs exclusively have Ehrhart polynomial roots with real part -1/2. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNorm Bounds for Ehrhart Polynomial Roots (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2126042_Norm_Bounds_for_Ehrhart_Polynomial_Roots)\nciteturn12search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; As a side effect this confirms a special case of a conjecture of Braun. ... (Braun, [6]) If f (t) is a degree d SNN polynomial, then all the roots of f (t) lie inside the closed disc with center −1 2 and radius d(d − 1 2 ... ... A proof of this theorem appeared in [6]; we will obtain it in this dissertation as a special case of the following more general theorem. ... This improves on the previously best known bound n and complements a recent result of Braun where it is shown that the norm of a root of an Ehrhart polynomial is at most of order n^2.\n\n# Norm Bounds for Ehrhart Polynomial Roots\n\n  * February 2006\n  * Discrete & Computational Geometry 39(1-3)\n\nDOI:10.1007/s00454-006-1297-0\n\nAuthors:\n\nBenjamin Braun\n\nBenjamin Braun\n\n## Abstract\n\nM. Beck, J. De Loera, M. Develin, J. Pfeifle and R. Stanley found that the roots of the Ehrhart polynomial of a d-dimensional lattice polytope are bounded above in norm by 1+(d+1)!. We provide an improved bound which is quadratic in d and applies to a larger family of polynomials. Comment: 3 pages, to appear in Discrete and Computational Geometry\n\n\n## Citations (25)\n\n## References (13)\n\n... That was done by constructing polytopes whose Ehrhart polynomials indicate a quadratic bound rather than a linear one. The quadratic growth of the bound was confirmed in [4]: for an Ehrhart polynomial of degree d, the roots all lie in a disc of radius d(d − 1 2 ) around the point − 1 2 in the complex plane. An important subclass of lattice polytopes are the reflexive polytopes which rose to popularity after Batyrev noticed their connection to string theory in [1]. ...\n\n... In Section 2, we will review some preliminaries on Ehrhart theory and interlacing theory. In Section 3, we present a smaller, but still quadratic, bound than the more general one found in [4]. ...\n\n... Remark 3.7. The following table compares the roots − 1 2 + β √ -1 of the standard reflexive simplices with the bounds from Theorem 3.5, [5], and [4]. ...\n\nProperties of Ehrhart Polynomials whose Roots Lie on the Critical Line\n\nPreprint\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jul 2022\n\n  * Max Kölbl\n\nWe study a class of polynomials that has all of its roots on the critical line and shares many properties with Ehrhart polynomials. Braun showed that the roots of Ehrhart polynomials are bounded quadratically and Higashitani provided examples for polytopes whose Ehrhart polynomial roots come close to this bound. In the case of polytopes which have their roots on the critical line, we present an improved bound. As a side effect this confirms a special case of a conjecture of Braun.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\n... There exists a certain counterexample of dimension 15. Braun [5] gives the best possible norm bound of roots of Ehrhart polynomials with O(d 2 ). ...\n\n... On many results of the studies on roots of Ehrhart polynomials, Stanley's nonnegativity of δ-vectors [17] plays a crucial role. (For example, see [4,5] and [6].) Derived from the definition [6, Definition 1.2], we define the following polynomial. ...\n\n... By (5) and (6) together with the norm bound [5], we also obtain the following Finally, we prove the following. All the real numbers in [− d 2 , d 2 − 1] can be realized as roots of SSNN polynomials of degree d. ...\n\n\n... Theorem 3.6. (Braun, [6]) If f (t) is a degree d SNN polynomial, then all the roots of f (t) lie inside the closed disc with center −1 2 and radius d(d − 1 2 ...\n\n... A proof of this theorem appeared in [6]; we will obtain it in this dissertation as a special case of the following more general theorem. Theorem 3.7. ...\n\n... The fact that − 1 2 is the center of the disc containing the roots of any degree d SNN polynomial has a heuristic explanation, as noted in [6]. All the polynomials in ...\n\nEhrhart theory for lattice polytopes /\n\n\nShow abstract\n\n... In a recent paper Braun [8] proved that the roots of an Ehrhart polynomial lie inside the disc with center −1/2 and radius n(n − 1)/2. The above theorem shows that this bound is essentially tight and improves on the former best known bound of order n [2, Theorem 1.3]. ...\n\nNotes on the roots of Ehrhart polynomials\n\nPreprint\n\n  * Jun 2006\n\n  * Christian Bey\n  * Martin Henk\n  * Jörg M. Wills\n\nWe determine lattice polytopes of smallest volume with a given number of interior lattice points. We show that the Ehrhart polynomials of those with one interior lattice point have largest roots with norm of order n^2, where n is the dimension. This improves on the previously best known bound n and complements a recent result of Braun where it is shown that the norm of a root of an Ehrhart polynomial is at most of order n^2. For the class of 0-symmetric lattice polytopes we present a conjecture on the smallest volume for a given number of interior lattice points and prove the conjecture for crosspolytopes. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEHRHART POLYNOMIAL ROOTS OF REFLEXIVE POLYTOPES 17 (https://kasprzyk.work/research/pdf/CL_polytopes.pdf)\nciteturn12search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.6 years ago; EHRHART POLYNOMIAL ROOTS OF REFLEXIVE POLYTOPES 17 ... Remembering to exclude the trivial action, a simple counting argument gives us (21 · 10 · 2 − 12)/2 + 11 = 215 distinct group actions. ... [6] Benjamin Braun and Mike Develin.\nEHRHART POLYNOMIAL ROOTS OF REFLEXIVE POLYTOPES 17\n\nthis involution fixes exactly 3 · 2 · 2 = 12 cases (including the trivial action). Remembering to exclude the trivial action, a simple counting argument gives us (21 · 10 · 2 − 12)/2 + 11 = 215 distinct group actions. Condition (6.8) reduces the 215 possible group actions to just 58 candidates; we call the set of such candidates 𝒢. Remembering that our decision to use the weights (1^6, 2^3, 3^2) to zero α0 was a choice, and that we could just have validly chosen to set αi = 0 for any i ∈ {0, . . . , 5} (i.e. any i such that λi = 1), we have that for any ⟨1/3(α0, . . . , α10)⟩ ∈ 𝒢 we need ⟨1/3(α0 − αiλ0, . . . , α10 − αiλ10)⟩ ∈ 𝒢, for each i ∈ {0, . . . , 5}, where now we regard groups as being defined only up to permutations of {0, . . . , 10} that fix λσi = λi. This gives us exactly one possible group action, generated by\n\n1/3 (0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 1, 2).\n\nFinally, we compute the resulting polytope P and check that the δ-vector agrees. Up to isomorphism,\n\nP = conv{e1, . . . , e9, (1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3), (−4, −5, −2, −3, −4, −1, −2, −2, −3, −3)},\n\nwhere ei is the i-th standard basis element, and P has δ-vector (1, 1, 1, 1, 9, 28, 9, 1, 1, 1, 1).\n\n**Acknowledgments.** AH is partially supported by a JSPS Fellowship for Young Scientists and by JSPS Grant-in-Aid for Young Scientists (B) #17K14177. AK is supported by EPSRC Fellowship EP/N022513/1.\n\n**REFERENCES**\n\n[1] Victor V. Batyrev. Dual polyhedra and mirror symmetry for Calabi-Yau hypersurfaces in toric varieties. *J. Algebraic Geom.*, 3(3):493–535, 1994.  \n[2] M. Beck, J. A. De Loera, M. Develin, J. Pfeifle, and R. P. Stanley. Coefficients and roots of Ehrhart polynomials. In *Integer points in polyhedra—geometry, number theory, algebra, optimization*, volume 374 of *Contemp. Math.*, pages 15–36. Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 2005.  \n[3] Christian Bey, Martin Henk, and Jörg M. Wills. Notes on the roots of Ehrhart polynomials. *Discrete Comput. Geom.*, 38(1):81–98, 2007.  \n[4] A. A. Borisov and L. A. Borisov. Singular toric Fano